{"caseName":"Brogan v. United States","citation":"522 U.S. 398","court":"scotus","dateFiled":"1998-01-26","sourceUrl":"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/118168/brogan-v-united-states/","opinionExcerpt":"By its terms, 18 U.S.C. § 1001 covers 'any' false statement—that is, a false statement 'of whatever kind,' United States v. Gonzales, 520 U.S. 1, 5 (1997). We cannot find any basis in the text of § 1001 for an 'exculpatory no' exception."}